WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.620
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:09.120
Today I will show you a research&nbsp;
paper that I can hardly believe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:15.240
exists. And it is about an amazing voice&nbsp;
cloning paper from Microsoft Research.

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:21.000
What does that mean? Well, voice&nbsp;
cloning means that an AI listens to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:25.920
us speaking, and then, we write a piece&nbsp;
of text, and it says it in our voice.

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:32.400
To see what that looks like, this is a previous&nbsp;
work from NVIDIA that was able to do that. Let’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:42.540
listen to Jamil recording these voice snippets.&nbsp;
Okay, so how much of this do we need to train this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:50.520
earlier AI? Well, not the entire life recordings&nbsp;
of the test subject, but much less, only 30&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:58.080
minutes of these voice samples. The technique asks&nbsp;
us to say these sentences and analyzes the timbre,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:05.880
prosody and the rhythm of our voice, which is&nbsp;
quite a task. And, what can it do afterwards?

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:12.900
Well, it creates an AI Jamil that can now&nbsp;
say a scholarly message for you that I wrote.

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:30.960
Now, hold on to your papers, because&nbsp;
Microsoft has a cracking new paper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:35.460
with an AI they call VALL-E&nbsp;
that is able to do the same,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:45.240
but not in 30 minutes. Not even in 3 minutes.&nbsp;
It can do it in 3 seconds. Yes, that’s right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:50.280
all it needs is a three-second snippet&nbsp;
of our voice, and it promises that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:57.480
can clone it from that. And it gets crazier.&nbsp;
Much crazier, I will show you how in a moment.

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:04.980
First, in goes a 3-second voice sample of us&nbsp;
saying something. This does not need to match&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:14.820
the text prompt here. This is going to be used for&nbsp;
the learning. Now that it has learned this voice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:28.860
here is what a previous technique could do in&nbsp;
terms of cloning. And here is the new method.&nbsp;

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:45.840
Wow. The phrasing and timing are so much better.&nbsp;
But actually, how good is it? How do we even know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:52.800
if this is really better? Well, easy, we can ask&nbsp;
this person to read this prompt in their voice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.800 --> 00:03:08.400
hide it from the AI, and compare side by side.&nbsp;
Listen. AI goes first. Real person second.

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:23.460
This is absolutely incredible. This truly&nbsp;
feels like we are seeing history in the making.

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:39.060
Here is another example. A speaker prompt for&nbsp;
learning. And the new technique. I love this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:52.800
it even has a little personality in there. And&nbsp;
the true human voice. I have to say this is even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:59.580
better, but the AI was also out of this world&nbsp;
compared to what we were able to do before.

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:05.400
Here is another one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:18.000
and then, we proceed to my favorite part of&nbsp;
the paper, three advanced features. So good.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:25.320
First, variety. This new technique can generate&nbsp;
several variants of speech for the same prompt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:30.360
so we can listen to them and choose&nbsp;
the one that we like best. Listen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:39.000
how it puts the emphasis on&nbsp;
different words. Loving it.

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:44.400
Two, get this, it can also listen to&nbsp;
the 3-second example of our voice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:58.260
and preserve its emotions too. Here is&nbsp;
an angry one. And here is a sleepy one.&nbsp;

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:10.380
I really don’t know what to say.&nbsp;
This is absolutely incredible.

00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:16.740
And three, it can also maintain not&nbsp;
only the emotions, but the ambiance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:22.020
and acoustic environment our sample&nbsp;
has been recorded in. My favorite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:32.280
is this sample which sounds like an old&nbsp;
crackly phone conversation. Like this. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:41.340
can it clone this kind of sample too?&nbsp;
Let’s listen together. My goodness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:48.060
I did not think that this was possible at all,&nbsp;
let alone from a tiny tiny 3-second example.

00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:55.320
And just imagine the possibilities. This could&nbsp;
bring back people who are not with us anymore and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.320 --> 00:06:02.820
have them read us books and bedtime stories.&nbsp;
How amazing is that? Maybe we will have the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:09.840
incredible Isaac Asimov himself reading his own&nbsp;
robot books to us soon. What a time to be alive!

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:17.340
And remember, NVIDIA’s previous technique needed&nbsp;
30 minutes for this, and now just one more paper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:25.560
down the line, and 600 times less information is&nbsp;
enough to create voice samples of this quality.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:30.960
600 times less! And just imagine what we will be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:34.740
able to do two more papers&nbsp;
down the line. My goodness.

00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:41.520
Now, of course, this is a great research&nbsp;
paper, so what does that mean? Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:48.480
that means that it has a thorough evaluation&nbsp;
section. So let’s pop the hood and have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:54.060
look. Whoa. That is really nice! I’ll try to&nbsp;
explain this. We first compare against previous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.060 --> 00:06:59.760
techniques in terms of the word error rate&nbsp;
metric. The two variants of the new technique&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:08.340
come out the absolute best. However, correctness&nbsp;
by itself is not enough, we also wish to know if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.340 --> 00:07:15.300
the new samples are not only correct, but also&nbsp;
similar to the speaker in the input sample.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.300 --> 00:07:23.280
So, is it? Wow. The new technique comes&nbsp;
out the absolute best on the two things at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:29.820
the same time. However, wait. These two are&nbsp;
some ancient techniques from long-long ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.820 --> 00:07:37.740
right? Nope. YourTTS is from the same&nbsp;
year as this paper. And so is AudioLM.

00:07:38.340 --> 00:07:44.580
This is so much progress in research&nbsp;
in so little time. Now that truly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.580 --> 00:07:49.560
feels like history in the making.&nbsp;
So, welcome to Two Minute Papers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.560 --> 00:07:54.840
land of the Fellow Scholars where we&nbsp;
look at tables and make happy noises.

